WEBVTT

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:00.768
Trust itself, it's like,
it's intrinsic to relationships.

00:07:00.792 --> 00:07:03.434
AG: Yeah, of course. I would
not argue with that.

00:07:03.458 --> 00:07:05.643
There's something
that's a little odd, though,

00:07:05.667 --> 00:07:07.768
about defining power
in terms of dependence.

00:07:07.792 --> 00:07:11.226
So there is a strong
sociological tradition of saying,

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.309
"Look, the power in a relationship is
how much do I depend on you

00:07:14.333 --> 00:07:15.976
or how much do you depend on me."

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.184
In an organization,
though, that would suggest

00:07:18.208 --> 00:07:20.434
that the CEO is the least powerful person,

00:07:20.458 --> 00:07:23.976
because the CEO is completely dependent
on everyone else to do their jobs

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.809
or else the organization fails.

00:07:25.833 --> 00:07:28.934
And yet that's not how we
traditionally look at power.

00:07:28.958 --> 00:07:30.684
EP: We look traditionally wrong.